  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Easterly gap winds will weaken through the morning, turning\n   westerly by this afternoon\n\n-  Widespread rain and mountain snow above 7,000 feet late \n   tonight into early Monday, mainly through central Idaho and \n   northwest Montana.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\nA Pacific storm system is moving inland today and will track into \nnorthwest Montana tonight, bringing showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to north-central Idaho early this morning. Ahead of \nthis system, strong easterly winds are currently peaking across \nwestern Montana, particularly near the Whitefish, Swan, and \nMission mountains, as well as Hellgate Canyon. Localized gusts of \n40 to 50 mph will create choppy lake conditions and blustery \nbackcountry weather before shifting to breezy westerly winds this \nafternoon.\n\nThe heaviest precipitation will target north-central Idaho and \nnorthwest Montana, bringing up to an inch of rain in the \nmountains and lighter amounts in the valleys. Snow levels will \ndrop to near 7,000 feet tonight, resulting in light accumulations \nalong the Continental Divide and creating cold, raw conditions \nthat pose a hypothermia risk for backcountry recreationists. \nFurther south and east, including Missoula, rainfall will be light\nand spotty. Conversely, southwest Montana will remain dry with \ngusty southwest winds, prompting a Red Flag Warning for critical \nfire weather conditions this afternoon and evening.\n\nThis weather system will exit the region by Tuesday, making way \nfor a generally warmer and drier pattern through mid-week, \nalthough a few stray showers remain possible. Decreasing cloud \ncover on Tuesday morning will likely result in the coldest \ntemperatures of the fall season so far. Sub-freezing temperatures \nare expected in the higher valleys of southwest Montana and along \nthe Continental Divide, while frost will be a concern for the \nMissoula and Bitterroot valleys, as well as the higher valleys of \nthe Clearwater Mountains. Recent model trends suggest another low \npressure system may cut off along the west coast Wednesday night \nand then slowly drift through western Montana and north central \nIdaho Thursday into Friday. The moisture feed with this system \nlooks relatively dry, but precipitation chances may trend upward \nagain in this time frame. This scenario appears in about 50 to 60 \npercent of model solutions, but overall confidence in the details \nremains low.",
